# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# cografya_web — environment variable template
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for local development. Never commit real values.
# These vars are validated at boot via zod (CONVENTIONS §3 — no unvalidated process.env
# reads): `lib/env.ts` for the client-exposed `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` ones, `lib/env.server.ts`
# for the server-only ones (a secret belongs ONLY in the server schema).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Absolute site origin (no trailing slash). Drives metadataBase + canonical /
# hreflang / sitemap URLs. Defaults to http://localhost:3000 when unset for local
# dev; MUST be set to the real production domain before deploy.
#
# TWO MORE SERVER-SIDE JOBS AS OF UYELIK-03 (lib/auth/transport.server.ts,
# lib/auth/cookies.ts) — a wrong value here now breaks LOGIN, not just canonicals:
#   · Origin allowlist: every POST to /api/auth/** must present this exact value in its
#     `Origin` header or the request is refused (403) before any api call.
#   · The `Secure` cookie attribute: derived from this URL's hostname, INVERTED on purpose
#     (Secure by default, opt out only for a loopback host — localhost/127.0.0.1/[::1]).
#     Getting this wrong fails LOUD in both directions: a mismatched Origin 403s every
#     request, and the inverted rule never silently downgrades a real deployment to a
#     non-Secure cookie.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Origin of the cografya_api the SSG/ISR data layer fetches from. SERVER-ONLY
# (not NEXT_PUBLIC_ — never exposed to the browser). Validated by lib/env.server.ts.
# Defaults to the local api dev port (3001). MUST be the real internal api origin
# in production. If the api is unreachable during `next build`, province pages
# fall back to on-demand ISR at runtime (the build still succeeds).
API_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# OPTIONAL shared secret that exempts our SERVER-SIDE api reads from the api's
# global rate limit (cografya_api TrustedClientThrottlerGuard). Sent in the
# `x-internal-request-token` header by lib/api/client.ts; must match the api's own
# INTERNAL_REQUEST_TOKEN exactly, byte for byte.
# SERVER-ONLY SECRET — never give it a NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, never read it from a
# client component, never commit a real value. Fail-closed: leave the line commented
# out and no header is sent (every request is throttled, i.e. today's behaviour).
# A full `next build` AGAINST A LIVE, THROTTLING API needs it, so it is a first-deploy
# checklist item on the build environment (CI's build job passes without it because no
# api is reachable there and the data layer degrades to on-demand ISR).
#
# HOW TO MINT IT — `openssl rand -hex 32`. One line, nothing else:
#   · An EMPTY assignment is invalid (>= 32 chars) — comment the line out instead.
#   · NEVER a wrapped `openssl rand -base64 64`: openssl breaks base64 at 64 columns, so
#     the value carries a newline. `lib/env.server.ts` rejects a newline at boot — but an
#     UNQUOTED multiline value in a .env FILE never even reaches the schema: @next/env
#     silently truncates it to its first line (measured), and that truncated half then
#     mismatches the api while looking correct in both stores. No code-level validation
#     can see it, which is why this line is the control. Keep the value to ONE line of
#     visible ASCII: no spaces, no quoted-newline tricks.
#   · Rotating it changes every Next fetch-cache key (request headers are part of the
#     key), so the first build after a rotation refetches everything from the api — the
#     one build that most needs the exemption already in place on both sides.
# INTERNAL_REQUEST_TOKEN=

# Google Analytics 4 measurement ID (e.g. G-XXXXXXXXXX). Public — client-exposed.
# Declared, not wired yet.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=

# Google Search Console HTML-tag verification token. Public — client-exposed.
# Declared, not wired yet.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GSC_VERIFICATION=
